u}$ decay using a dataset collected in 2016. This dataset, comprising 21.5 times 10^11 clastic proton interactions, is the largest ever recorded. A detailed analysis was undertaken to identify the $K^+ightarrow\pi^+
u ar{
u}$ decay by means of topological and kinematic selection, resulting in 15 candidate events. From these data, the branching ratio for the mentioned decay is estimated with a 90% confidence level to be less than $10^{-10}$, which is in accordance with the Standard Model's expectations and recent results from the K2K and BELLE experiments.
